  "query_preview": "..A broad Pacific Northwest low is centered over\ncentral Nevada this evening, with the front half slowly making its\nway across Utah and southwest Wyoming. Its instability is\ncombining with remnant moisture from what was Tropical Storm\nRachel to bring fairly widespread coverage of precipitation,\nparticularly for the western half of the area. Much of the state\nis waterlogged, and totals keep going up. However, the more steady\nnature of this rainfall has thus far not brought any additional\nflooding issues. Temperatures remain on the cool side, with maxes\naveraging 10F below seasonal normals.\n\nThe low's centered is expected to make a southeasterly trek to the\nArizona/Nevada/Utah triple point Tuesday morning before tracking\nsteadily eastward across the Desert Southwest through early\nWednesday. This is not the direct hit from the low that guidance\nhas been predicting in prior days, but it should still keep fairly\nwidespread precipitation going over the forecast area throughout\nTuesday with steadily lowering snow levels. That being said, snow\nlevels are already a bit lower than forecast with snow being\nobserved at the top of Little Cottonwood, so have opted to have\nthe going Winter Weather Advisory for that zone start right away\nas opposed to tomorrow morning. QPF forecast has trended downward\na bit given the change in the low's expected track, but still\nanticipate the snow in the Wasatch and the Uintas to be\nsignificant for the early season. Some showers may linger over\neastern portions of the area from the low as late as Wednesday.\nHowever, latest model solutions are a bit quicker pulling the\nsystem out of the area.\n\nThe low is already bringing noticeably colder air with it and this\nwill continue to filter in throughout Tuesday. Some outlying\nvalleys, including the Cache Valley, could touch 32F as early as\ntomorrow morning. However, the freezing temperatures will be a bit\nmore widespread Wednesday and Thursday mornings in the absence of\nsignificant cloud cover. \n\nAs the low finally exits, the ridge off the California coast will\nbecome the dominant weather feature over the forecast area. This\nwill bring dry conditions with a slow but steady warming trend\nthrough early next week.",
